  "raw": {
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Adjunct-Instructor--Business-in-Practice_JR10364": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor, Business in Practice",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The University of New Hampshire’s Peter T. Paul College of Business and Economics is seeking an adjunct instructor to teach in one or more of our business administration and /or economics programs. Adjunct appointments are on a course-by-course basis; renewal may be possible depending on department needs and teaching performance. Duties/Responsibilities: Develop and deliver engaging course content in the assigned course(s),consistent with curricular guidance provided by the department chair and/or course coordinator. Foster an inclusive and collaborative learning environment that encourages creativity and critical thinking. Provide at least one scheduled office hour per week, either on campus or online, for each course. Utilize the university’s learning management system to make course information, resources, and feedback available to students in a timely manner. Evaluate student performance through assignments, projects, presentations, and examinations in accordance with university policies. Stay current with new developments and resources related to course content. Participate in periodic meetings with college faculty leadership as needed. Requirements: Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: Applicants should have either a related Master’s or Ph.D. degree. A related Bachelor’s degree and extensive work experience may substitute for the advanced degree requirement. Experience and interest in training and mentoring young professionals, either formally or informally. Applicants are expected to meet the College’s standards for faculty qualifications, typically as an Instructional Practitioner, with respect to AACSB standards. The applicant is expected to be committed to excellent teaching and be engaged in continued professional development/practice. Preferred Qualifications: Prior recent experience in secondary or college-level classroom teaching. Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents - additional documents can be added to the Application Questions section of the application)   Resume/CV Compensation Pay Range: $3,000 The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Period Activity"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Research-Assistant-Professor_JR10052": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The University of New Hampshire's Community Literacy Center (CLC), housed within the Department of Education in the College of Liberal Arts (COLA), is a resource for PreK–12 learners, families, and educators that supports the literacy learning of area children through collaboration with schools, families, and community organizations. This grant-funded position is focused primarily on research, with the Research Assistant Professor operating at a high level of scholarly independence and expertise across a portfolio of literacy-related initiatives. The Research Assistant Professor will lead and contribute to research activities supporting the SLATE program — a U.S. Department of Education-funded initiative combining near-peer tutoring for middle school students with professional learning for teachers to enhance achievement in literacy, writing, and STEM — as well as the CLC's community-based research projects, including the Multilingual Book to Art program and Community Storytime, a program that brings UNH students to local community sites to read with children, lead book-inspired activities, and provide books to children in partnership with organizations such as food pantries, libraries, and afterschool programs. As part of their grant-funded work, the Research Assistant Professor will advise and mentor graduate students who are funded through the grant. Additionally, as part of their CLC role, the Research Assistant Professor will provide mentored practicum opportunities for UNH Education students through Community Storytime and SLATE activities. The Research Assistant Professor will support the broader programmatic and community engagement endeavors of the CLC, contributing to its mission of connecting educators, pre-service teachers, youth, families, and UNH faculty around the shared goal of excellent literacy learning throughout New Hampshire. This position is not considered essential staff and is not required to report to work during periods of university curtailment. Duties/Responsibilities SLATE Research and Program Support (85%) Design, conduct, and oversee research studies related to the SLATE tutoring program, including data collection, analysis, and interpretation. Disseminate research findings through scholarly publications, conference presentations, and reports to funding agencies. Support UNH student tutors in lesson planning and instructional practice, providing mentorship and guidance. Collaborate with SLATE team members, partner schools, and community organizations to support program implementation and a shared research agenda. Advise and mentor doctoral SLATE-funded doctoral students. CLC Community-Based Research and Program Support (10%) Lead and contribute to community-based research projects within the CLC, including the Multilingual Book to Art program and Community Storytime. Support the implementation and ongoing development of CLC community engagement programs in partnership with community organizations, schools, and families. Service and Grant Support (5%) Contribute to grant reporting, documentation, and compliance activities associated with funded projects. Participate in departmental and CLC meetings, committees, and initiatives as appropriate. Support future grant development efforts related to literacy research and community engagement.    Requirements Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: Ph.D. in Education   Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Knowledge of current research and evidence-based practices in literacy instruction, including foundational literacy skills and reading and writing development across PreK–12 settings. Knowledge of issues and best practices related to multilingual learners and underrepresented student populations in literacy and STEM education. Familiarity with quantitative and qualitative research methods, with expertise in community-based research practices including practitioner research, action research, community action research, and research-practice partnerships (RPPs). Skill in analyzing and interpreting research data and translating findings into accessible formats for diverse audiences, including educators, community partners, and funding agencies. Skill in scholarly writing and the ability to produce publications, grant reports, and conference presentations. Skill in grant writing and securing external funding, including identifying funding opportunities and contributing to proposal development. Ability to build and maintain collaborative relationships with K–12 schools, community organizations, families, and university colleagues. Ability to mentor and support undergraduate and graduate students in research and tutoring contexts. Ability to manage multiple projects simultaneously and meet deadlines across a complex, grant-funded work environment. Ability to work effectively with diverse communities, including families and children from historically underserved backgrounds. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to represent the CLC and SLATE program professionally in community and academic settings.   Preferred Qualifications: Experience conducting research in community-based or K–12 school settings, particularly in literacy or STEM education contexts. Experience working with multilingual learners and/or underserved student populations. Experience mentoring or supervising undergraduate or graduate students in research or tutoring contexts. Experience building and maintaining partnerships with community organizations, schools, or families. Demonstrated record of scholarly publication and/or conference presentation in literacy, education, or a related field. Experience contributing to grant writing and/or management of externally funded projects. Familiarity with the SLATE program or similar cross-disciplinary literacy and STEM initiatives. Experience with program evaluation or assessment in educational settings. Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents)   Resume/CV Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration. Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed.   Compensation Pay Range: $65,000 - $74,658 The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate’s qualifications. The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Research-Professor_JR10197": {
      "title": "Research Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app Under the general direction of the Department Chair and the COLSA Dean, the Research Professor position will serve as a senior scholarly leader within MCBS. The position requires an exceptionally high level of specialized research skills, a proven record of independent scholarship, and substantial expertise in securing and managing large-scale external grants and contracts. Entrusted with a broad scope of operational and fiscal responsibility, the Research Professor functions as a principal investigator, managing substantial budgets, overseeing advanced laboratory or field environments, and directing the mentorship and professional development of graduate students and research staff. Administratively, the position embeds within the MCBS department’s broader intellectual framework to elevate the university's research profile and focuses on project-driven scholarship and specialized graduate education. Under the UNH Curtailed Operations Policy, this position is not generally designated as essential staff required to physically report to campus during university curtailments. However, because research continuity and specific grant-funded responsibilities may vary, individuals must verify specific campus access permissions or remote-work expectations directly with their department chair or Dean. Duties/Responsibilities Research (80%) Lead and contribute to the establishment and operations of the Center for Antimicrobial Detection, Ecology, Characterization, and Innovation in New Hampshire (CADECI-NH). Investigate the ecological underpinnings of antimicrobial resistance (AMR) on farms and at the wildland-farm interface. Drive innovation incubator activities focused on sensing and characterizing known and novel antimicrobials and antimicrobial resistance genes. Generate data and develop tools to inform and guide best practices for antimicrobial stewardship on farms. Advance research with broad implications for human health, agricultural health, food chain security, aquaculture, and wildlife and environmental health. Build and maintain an intentional, collaborative network of subject matter experts spanning the biological, chemical, and environmental sciences. Lead and participate in interdisciplinary research initiatives that bridge biological, chemical, and environmental sciences. Translate foundational ecological knowledge into applied innovations and practical solutions for antimicrobial stewardship. Contribute to the adaptability of the center's research agenda to address emerging questions across biological, chemical, and environmental disciplines. Establish and maintain an independently funded research program aligned with the department's strategic priorities in biomedical sciences. Pursue and secure competitive federal and private research funding through grant applications and sponsored research initiatives. Lead and participate in collaborative, interdisciplinary research initiatives within and beyond the department. Strengthen and expand research partnerships across the Department of Molecular, Cellular & Biomedical Sciences (MCBS), the College of Life Sciences and Agriculture, and university research centers. Mentor and supervise graduate and undergraduate researchers in laboratory and research settings. Enhance opportunities for student training and workforce development in the biomedical sciences. Publish original research findings in high-impact, peer-reviewed scientific journals. Contribute to the broader scientific community through presentations, collaborations, and professional engagement. Contribute to and support the department's research infrastructure and shared resources. Advance the department's mission to expand research excellence, increase external funding, and strengthen UNH's national reputation in biomedical research.   Teaching and Service (20%) Teach one course in MCBS/year in your area of expertise in coordination with the Chair. Serve on departmental, college, or university committees and participate in faculty meetings and strategic planning efforts. Support departmental seminar series, symposia, and research events. Review manuscripts for peer-reviewed scientific journals and serve as a reviewer for federal and private grant funding agencies. Participate in professional scientific societies and organizations. Contribute to graduate program activities such as thesis committees, qualifying exams, and student recruitment. Support diversity, equity, and inclusion initiatives within the department and broader scientific community. Engage in science outreach and STEM education activities to promote biomedical research to the broader public. Represent UNH and the department at regional and national scientific conferences and events.   Requirements Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: PhD (or advanced graduate degree such as MD, DVM, DO) and experience in Microbiology, Ecology, Environmental Science, Chemistry, Biochemistry, or a closely related field. Demonstrated experience in antimicrobial resistance research, microbial ecology, or a related biomedical/environmental science field. Experience designing and conducting independent research projects. Demonstrated experience pursuing or securing external research funding (federal or private). Proficiency in laboratory techniques relevant to antimicrobial detection, characterization, or microbial ecology. Experience with data analysis and interpretation. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively in interdisciplinary research settings. Strong record of peer-reviewed publications and scientific presentations. Experience mentoring or supervising graduate or undergraduate researchers.   Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: In-depth knowledge of antimicrobial resistance mechanisms, microbial ecology, and environmental microbiology. Knowledge of federal and private research funding landscapes, including NIH, NSF, and USDA grant mechanisms. Knowledge of best practices for antimicrobial stewardship in agricultural and environmental settings. Knowledge of biosafety protocols and regulatory requirements for biomedical and environmental research. Strong scientific writing skills for grant applications, peer-reviewed publications, and technical reports. Proficiency in molecular, microbiological, and/or analytical chemistry laboratory techniques. Skills in bioinformatics, genomics, or data science as applied to AMR research. Strong organizational and project management skills to lead multidisciplinary research initiatives. Ability to build and sustain collaborative relationships across disciplines, departments, and institutions. Ability to mentor and train graduate and undergraduate researchers effectively. Ability to communicate complex scientific concepts to diverse audiences including scientists, policymakers, and the public. Ability to adapt research agenda to emerging scientific questions and funding opportunities. Ability to contribute to a diverse, equitable, and inclusive research environment.   Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated record of securing competitive external research funding as a principal investigator (PI) or co-investigator (co-PI). Experience leading or contributing to large-scale, multidisciplinary or multi-institutional research centers or initiatives. Strong publication record in high-impact, peer-reviewed scientific journals in relevant fields. Experience with One Health research approaches that integrate human, animal, and environmental health perspectives. Familiarity with agricultural systems, farm ecology, aquaculture, or wildlife biology. Experience with advanced molecular techniques such as metagenomics, whole genome sequencing, or high-throughput screening. Demonstrated commitment to mentoring diverse student populations and fostering an inclusive research environment. Established network of collaborators in the biological, chemical, and environmental sciences. Experience engaging with policymakers, industry partners, or community stakeholders on antimicrobial stewardship issues. Compensation Pay Range: $116,000 The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate’s qualifications. The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Cardiac-Rehab-Program-Adjunct-Faculty_JR10306": {
      "title": "Cardiac Rehab Program Adjunct Faculty",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app This is a two-part position to cover temporary leave in Spring of 2027 within the Exercise Science Undergraduate Program in the Department of Kinesiology for: Direction of the UNH Cardiac Rehabilitation and Prevention Program and teaching coverage for the clinical course associated with the program (EXSC 795: Cardiac Rehab Practicum). Duties/Responsibilities: Clinical Teaching: EXSC 795 – Cardiac Rehab Practicum (1.5 units, 6 credits of teaching) Teach and oversee students as they develop the knowledge and clinical skills necessary to appropriately monitorand lead group and individualized exercise programming for individuals with cardiovascular disease and/ormultiple cardiovascular risk factors, including patients with complex comorbidities and orthopedic limitations. Provide instruction in clinical assessment, exercise prescription, exercise progression, patient monitoring, and interpretation of physiological responses to exercise. Provide instruction in medical record interpretation, review of relevant medical history, and daily patient status/update reporting. Instruct students in the recognition of abnormal signs/symptoms, exercise termination criteria, emergency procedures, and appropriate response to clinical events. Directly supervise students during clinical interactions and exercise sessions. Provide ongoing feedback and mentorship regarding students’ clinical performance. Manage the Canvas course site, including assignment management, updating due dates, and grading assignments in a timely manner. Other duties as assigned. Requirements: Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: Master’s Degree in Exercise Physiology, Exercise Science, Kinesiology, or other closely related field. Minimum of 5 years of clinical experience providing exercise assessment, prescription, and supervision for individuals with cardiovascular disease and/or multiple chronic disease risk factors. Required Licenses & Certifications: Current BLS/CPR/AED certification Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Knowledge : Cardiovascular disease (CVD) and risk factor management. Cardiac rehabilitation principles and standards. Exercise prescription, monitoring, interpretation of physiological responses, appropriate progression/regression of exercise. Cardiovascular pharmacology and medical management. Risk stratification, emergency response, and patient safety. Skills: Conducting clinical assessments and exercise testing appropriate for a cardiovascular population. Developing individualized exercise prescriptions and progression plans for individuals with CVD. Monitoring of patients during exercise and appropriately responding to changes in HR, BP, symptoms, and perceived exertion. Providing effective patient education and behavior change counseling related to CV risk reduction. Training and supervising undergraduate students, graduate assistants, and/or other personnel. Abilities: Provide clinical leadership for a medically supervised cardiovascular exercise program. Maintain a high-quality, evidence-based, patient-centered program. Monitor and develop students and emerging clinical exercise professionals. Adapt programming to meet the needs of individuals with varying levels of cardiovascular risk, health status, and functional capacity. Balance clinical, academic, administrative, and operational responsibilities. Preferred Qualifications: ACSM Certified Clinical Exercise Physiologist (ACSM-CEP). Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents - additional documents can be added to the Application Questions section of the application)   Resume/CV Cover Letter Contact Information for 3 Professional References Compensation Pay Range: Compensation is based on credit hours taught: 1-2 Credits $3,100 3-4 Credits $6,100 The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Period Activity"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Adjunct-Faculty_JR10270": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nPart time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The University of New Hampshire’s Peter T. Paul College of Business and Economics is seeking an adjunct instructor to teach in one or more of our business administration and /or economics programs. Adjunct appointments are on a course-by-course basis; renewal may be possible depending on department needs and teaching performance. The Peter T. Paul College is a nationally ranked and AACSB accredited business school in the state of New Hampshire with world-class academic programs in business administration, economics, and hospitality management. We are an inclusive community that respects and harnesses the power of diverse perspectives, identities, and experiences to enhance learning. The College has about 100 full-time faculty, approximately 2,400 students in our undergraduate majors, several hundred undergraduate minors, and over 300 students in graduate programs, including MBA (full-time and online), MS Accounting, MS Finance, MS Business Analytics, and MS and Ph.D. in Economics. Paul College faculty and students enjoy a state-of-the-art facility with 115,000 square feet of space and high-tech classrooms, a behavioral research laboratory, and breakout meeting rooms. Duties/Responsibilities Develop and deliver engaging course content in the assigned course(s), consistent with curricular guidance provided by the department chair and/or course coordinator. Foster an inclusive and collaborative learning environment that encourages creativity and critical thinking. Provide at least one scheduled office hour per week, either on campus or online, for each course. Utilize the university’s learning management system to make course information, resources, and feedback available to students in a timely manner. Evaluate student performance through assignments, projects, presentations, and examinations in accordance with university policies. Stay current with new developments and resources related to course content. Participate in periodic meetings with college faculty leadership as needed. Requirements Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: Applicants should have either a related Master’s or Ph.D. degree. A related Bachelor’s degree and extensive work experience may substitute for the advanced degree requirement. Experience and interest in training and mentoring young professionals, either formally or informally. Applicants are expected to meet the College’s standards for faculty qualifications, typically as an Instructional Practitioner, with respect to AACSB standards. The applicant is expected to be committed to excellent teaching and be engaged in continued professional development/practice. Preferred Qualifications: Prior recent experience in secondary or college-level classroom teaching. Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents - additional documents can be added to the Application Questions section of the application)   Resume/CV Compensation Pay Range: $3,000 The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Period Activity"
    },
