    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Political-Science_JR9426": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Political Science",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The Department of Political Science and International Affairs seeks a tenure-track assistant professor to pursue research in Legislative Studies and Public Policy in US politics and provide course instruction on the same at all levels of the department curriculum.  The assistant professor will be responsible for building a robust record of published research, delivering high-quality instruction to undergraduate and graduate students, and providing service within the University, as well as to the profession and the wider community. We seek candidates whose scholarly and teaching interests center on American politics, especially the legislative process and the creation of public policy.  Applicants may focus their research either on the U. S. Congress, and/or state legislatures. While subspeciality is open, the successful candidate will aid the department in offering courses on Congress, State and Local Government, and Public Policy, as well as the core introductory course in American politics. Additionally, the candidate will have the opportunity to develop advanced courses in their specific area of specialization. We are especially interested in applicants whose teaching or research can contribute complementary offerings to our department, such as: Media and Politics, Quantitative Data Analysis, Interest Groups and Political Parties, and Women and Politics.  We are especially interested in candidates whose teaching and research will contribute to the department’s commitment to inclusive excellence and is complimentary to existing expertise.  The standard teaching load consists of four courses distributed across two semesters. Duties/Responsibilities Scholarship (40%) Academic research in US legislative studies, public policy and related topics, including publications in peer-reviewed outlets and presentations at academic conferences. Teaching (35%) Instruction of students at various levels of the department and college curriculum, with an emphasis on US politics, legislative studies and public policy. Service (25%) Service in various capacities to the department, the College of Liberal Arts, the University of New Hampshire, the academic profession, and to the community in large. Examples of service include participation on committees, representing the department in the University Senate, organizing an academic conference, and engaging in research presentations to the public. Requirements Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: Ph.D. in Political Science with a subfield specialization in American Politics. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: The strongest applications will have evidence of productive scholarship. Preferred Qualifications: Teaching experience preferred. Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents) Resume/CV Statement of interest discussing research, teaching experience and prospective course contributions. Additionally, upload the following documents within the Application Questions section of the application: Publication or writing sample Three letters of reference Please email confidential letters of reference to katie.orazio@unh.edu Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration. Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed. Applications received by Saturday, October 17, 2026 will receive priority review. Compensation Pay Range: $74,658 The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate’s qualifications. The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor_JR9693": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The Department of Sociology at the University of New Hampshire (UNH) in Durham invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor position in technology and society. In addition to this primary area, candidates with expertise in one or more of the following are preferred: quantitative methods, computational sociology, emerging technologies, artificial intelligence (AI), sociology of health and illness, and environmental sociology. UNH seeks a social scientist who applies innovative empirical methods to examine how emerging technologies shape social institutions, inequalities, organizations, and communities, as well as how social forces shape the development, adoption, and use of those technologies. Responsibilities of the position include maintaining a productive research program, including seeking external funding; contributing to excellence in teaching and mentoring a diverse body of students; and providing engaged service to the department, university, and profession. The position carries a teaching load equivalent to four courses per academic year. The faculty member will teach introductory and advanced sociology courses as well as university-wide core curriculum courses. We particularly value faculty who engage students in research, including through opportunities to develop computational, methodological, and applied sociological skills. This is a full-time appointment beginning August 2027. The Department of Sociology at UNH is a collegial department with strengths in criminology, health, community and environmental sociology, and stratification. We are committed to rigorous sociological research and to preparing students for careers in academic, public-sector, nonprofit, and private-sector settings. The successful candidate will join a department that values collaborative research, student mentorship, and interdisciplinary engagement and will have opportunities to develop research and teaching collaborations with faculty and programs across UNH, including Justice Studies, Sustainability, International Affairs, and other programs throughout the university. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between AAUP-UNH and the University System of New Hampshire ( https://www.aaup-unh.org/contract/ ). Duties/Responsibilities:  Develop and maintain an active program of sociological research and pursue opportunities for external funding appropriate to their research agenda.  Develop courses in their area of expertise, such as technology and society, artificial intelligence and society, digital sociology, computational social science, or health, technology, and society.  Mentor students.  Work collaboratively with faculty in the sociology department and across UNH departments and programs.  Support departmental and university goals through service on committees or other administrative roles.   Required Qualifications :  Ph.D. in Sociology or a related field by the time of appointment; candidates who are ABD and anticipate completing the Ph.D. by August 2027 will be considered. Evidence of scholarly potential through publications, manuscripts, presentations, or other research accomplishments appropriate to career stage. Demonstrated commitment to teaching excellence and ability to contribute to the undergraduate sociology curriculum, including core courses, courses in their area of expertise, and other areas of departmental need. Evidence of collegiality and willingness to contribute to the department, university, and profession. Applicant Instructions Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online: Cover Letter A Curriculum Vitae Research Statement: A statement of your research accomplishments, current projects, and plans for developing a productive research program at UNH. Applicants may also describe opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration and external funding related to their research (no more than two pages). Teaching Statement: A statement of your approaches, experience, and philosophy regarding your teaching, learning, and mentoring (no more than two pages). Writing Sample: Please submit one writing sample that demonstrates your ability to conduct and communicate high-quality sociological research. The sample may be a published or unpublished article, dissertation chapter, book chapter, or comparable scholarly work. Contact information for three professional references Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration. Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed. Applications will be evaluated on a rolling basis with full consideration given to those received by October 9, 2026, or until the position is filled. Further information about the Department of Sociology may be found at https://cola.unh.edu/sociology . If you have questions, please contact Sociology.Search@unh.edu .  Compensation Pay Range $77,000 The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate’s qualifications. The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Clinical-Assistant-Professor_JR9763": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The Department of Occupational Therapy at the University of New Hampshire invites applications for a full-time clinical assistant  professor of occupational therapy with a potential start date of January 2027. The position is a 9-month academic year appointment with an additional part-time summer contract.  The Department of Occupational Therapy is housed in the College of Health and Human Services within UNH. UNH is a research-high, liberal arts, land grant institution. UNH provides a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. Duties/Responsibilities Clinical Instruction Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in alignment with ACOTE standards, course specific objectives and student outcomes. Develop and deliver course materials, assignments, exams, and clinical and applied learning activities Contribute to curriculum quality through course preparation, assessment, and continuous improvement of instructional materials Evaluate and document student progress and provide relevant and timely feedback to students. Participate in evaluation of courses, curricula, programs, and accreditation activities. Advise and mentor students, supporting academic progress and experiential learning Support admissions processes at undergraduate and graduate level Serve as a capstone project faculty mentor to OTD students Service   Consistent with the service mission of the University, the clinical faculty member is expected to engage in service activities at university, departmental, college, and professional levels.   Requirements Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: Terminal degree (PhD, OTD, ScD or similar) in occupational therapy At least 2 years of experience in OT clinical practice, preferably in one or more of the following areas: adult mental health, adult physical disabilities, geriatrics, leadership in OT practice. Required Licenses & Certifications: Active NH OT license or eligibility for NBCOT certification and eligibility for NH state OT license NH driver’s license or eligibility   Preferred Qualifications: An occupation-focused perspective, with teaching and/or fieldwork experience preferred Academic experience preferred, including experience teaching via online learning management systems Excellent communication, interpersonal, and leadership skills. Strong organizational, critical thinking, and problem-solving abilities. Experience working with college-age and non-traditional students. Skill in working with culturally diverse constituencies in the academic environment. Ability to cultivate external relationships with community leaders, institutions, and stakeholders. Demonstrated ability to work with faculty, staff, and students as well as community groups of diverse academic, socioeconomic, cultural, sexual orientation, disability and ethnic backgrounds.  Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents) Resume/CV Cover Letter Contact Information for 3 Professional References Teaching Philosophy Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration. Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed. Compensation Pay Range: $78,000 - $90,000 The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate’s qualifications. The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
