    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--MCBS-AI-Biochemistry_JR10370": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, MCBS—AI Biochemistry",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The College of Life Sciences and Agriculture (COLSA) at the University of New Hampshire (UNH) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor in the Department of Molecular, Cellular and Biomedical Sciences (MCBS). We seek an innovative scholar who develops and/or applies artificial intelligence and machine learning approaches to advance biochemistry and biomedical science. Areas of interest include protein structure, function, and engineering; molecular modeling and simulation; systems biology; multi-omics analysis; computational biochemistry; and AI-enabled therapeutic or drug discovery. The successful candidate will establish an externally funded research program, contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching, mentor students, and build scientific collaborations both within and beyond the Department of MCBS. The position offers opportunities to engage with UNH research infrastructure and collaborative programs, including the Center of Integrated Biomedical and Bioengineering Research (CIBBR), the Hubbard Center for Genome Studies (HCGS), the Research Computing Center, the Data Science Core, and NH-INBRE. Duties/Responsibilities Research and Scholarly Activity Establish and sustain an independent, nationally recognized, externally funded research program at the intersection of AI/machine learning and biochemistry or biomedical science. Develop and apply rigorous computational approaches to biological questions in areas such as protein modeling and engineering, molecular interactions, systems biology, multi-omics, biomarker discovery, or drug discovery. Publish research findings in peer-reviewed outlets and present work at scientific and professional meetings. Pursue competitive funding from federal agencies, foundations, industry, and other appropriate sponsors. Build collaborative research programs across MCBS, COLSA, and relevant UNH departments, centers, and external partners. Mentor undergraduate and graduate researchers and support their development as ethical, reproducible, and collaborative scientists. Teaching, Advising, and Mentorship Develop courses and learning modules that integrate artificial intelligence, machine learning, data science, and quantitative reasoning into biochemistry and the life sciences. Contribute to existing biochemistry and molecular bioscience curricula and participate in ongoing curriculum development. Advise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students, including students engaged in independent research and experiential learning. Use evidence-based and inclusive teaching practices that support students with varied academic, cultural, and professional backgrounds. Service and Professional Engagement Contribute service to the department, college, university, scientific community, and relevant professional organizations. Participate in faculty governance, student recruitment and retention, program assessment, and other activities that advance the academic mission. Help foster a collegial, inclusive, respectful, and team-oriented working and learning environment. Requirements Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: A Ph.D. in biochemistry, molecular biology, computational biology, bioinformatics, chemistry, computer science, data science, biomedical engineering, or a closely related field by the appointment start date. A record of research accomplishment demonstrating the use or development of AI/machine learning methods to address biochemical or biomedical questions. Evidence of the potential to establish an independent, externally funded research program. Evidence of the potential for effective undergraduate and graduate teaching, advising, and research mentorship. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities:   Expertise in AI/machine learning methods relevant to biochemical, molecular, structural, genomic, multi-omic, or drug-discovery research. Ability to connect computational methods with biologically meaningful questions, experimental evidence, and reproducible research practices. Strong written, oral, and interpersonal communication skills for working with students, collaborators, funding agencies, and professional audiences. Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research and educational environment. Commitment to responsible use of data and AI, including transparency, reproducibility, research integrity, and appropriate attention to bias and limitations. Commitment to creating an inclusive and respectful workplace and learning environment.   Preferred Qualifications: Postdoctoral research experience or equivalent advanced professional experience. A strong publication record in AI-enabled biochemistry, computational biology, structural biology, multi-omics, systems biology, molecular modeling, protein engineering, or computational drug discovery. Evidence of success in, or strong potential for, securing competitive external funding. Experience developing or teaching courses in biochemistry, computational biology, bioinformatics, data science, AI, or machine learning. Experience collaborating across computational and experimental disciplines and translating computational predictions into testable biological hypotheses. Experience mentoring students or trainees from a range of educational and personal backgrounds.  Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents - additional documents can be added to the Application Questions section of the application)   Resume/CV Cover Letter Contact Information for 3 Professional References Teaching Philosophy Research Statement   Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration. Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed.   Compensation Pay Range: $85,000 $95,000 The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate’s qualifications. The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Visiting-Assistant-Professor_JR10376": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The Department of Molecular, Cellular, and Biomedical Sciences (MCBS) in the College of Life Sciences and Agriculture (COLSA) at the University of New Hampshire (UNH) invites applications for a Visiting Assistant Professor. This is a full-time non-tenure-track faculty position with approximately 70% effort devoted to teaching and 30% to research and service. The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate and graduate instruction in biology, biochemistry, cell biology, genetics, or other areas of biomedical science; participate in student advising and departmental activities; and maintain scholarly engagement appropriate to the position. The successful candidate will be expected to teach four courses per academic year, with specific course assignments determined by departmental needs and the candidate's expertise. The appointment will be made on an annual basis and can be renewed for a total period of up to three years, in accordance with university policy and contingent upon satisfactory performance. Laboratory space can be provided to support research activities, contingent upon availability, appointee’s funding, and departmental needs. Start-up funding is not provided with this position. The successful candidate will be eligible to apply for internal and external research funding opportunities and is strongly encouraged to pursue collaborative and/or independent research grant proposals to secure research funding. Duties/Responsibilities Teaching (70%) The primary function of the Visiting Assistant Professor is to provide high-quality instruction across multiple undergraduate and graduate courses that are essential for the department’s educational mission. Responsibilities include: Teach four courses per academic year in areas relevant to the MCBS curriculum. Develop and deliver high-quality lectures, laboratory instruction, course materials, assessments, and other instructional activities, as appropriate to assigned courses. Contribute to existing courses and, where appropriate, develop or revise courses to address departmental and programmatic needs. Provide effective and timely assessment and feedback to students. Participate in activities that support student learning, retention, and academic success. Collaborate with other faculty to maintain consistency and quality across the MCBS curriculum. Teaching assignments may include courses in areas such as introductory biology, biochemistry, cell biology, cell culture, genetics, microbiology, or related disciplines, depending on departmental needs and the successful candidate's expertise. Research and Service (30%) The successful candidate will also be expected to maintain scholarly engagement and contribute service to the Department, College and University. Activities may include: Conduct independent and/or collaborative scholarly research in an area relevant to MCBS. Participate in ongoing research collaborations with MCBS or other UNH faculty. Advise undergraduate students for their academic and professional development. Mentor undergraduate students engaged in research. Contribute to scholarly products such as manuscripts, presentations, and, where appropriate, grant proposals. Participate in faculty meetings, student recruitment, departmental seminars, outreach, and other academic activities. Serve on departmental or program committees as appropriate to the appointment. Contribute to other service activities supporting the educational and research missions of MCBS and COLSA. Requirements Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: Ph.D. A Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree in biochemistry, molecular biology, cell biology, genetics, microbiology, neuroscience, biomedical sciences, or a closely related field by the anticipated start date. Demonstrated knowledge and expertise appropriate for teaching courses within the MCBS curriculum. Demonstrated research or scholarly experience appropriate to the discipline. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Knowledge of molecular, cellular, and biomedical sciences and related disciplines. Strong written and oral communication skills. Ability to use appropriate laboratory, computational, and instructional technologies. Ability to work effectively and collaboratively with students, faculty, and staff. Commitment to fostering an inclusive, supportive, and student-centered learning environment. Preferred Qualifications: Preference may be given to candidates with: Prior experience as instructor of record or substantial college-level teaching experience. Experience teaching both lecture and laboratory courses. Ability to teach across multiple areas of the MCBS curriculum. A record of effective mentoring of undergraduate and/or graduate students on research. Research expertise that complements the existing MCBS programs. Potential research collaboration with current MCBS faculty. Postdoctoral research experience. Evidence of scholarly productivity, including peer-reviewed publications. Experience contributing to collaborative and interdisciplinary research and educational programs. Track record of securing competitive research funding. Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents - additional documents can be added to the Application Questions section of the application)   Resume/CV Cover Letter Contact Information for 3 Professional References Research Statement Teaching Philosophy   Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration. Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed. Compensation Pay Range: $65,000 - $75,000 The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate’s qualifications. The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_JR10199": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The Furey Lab is looking to hire one Postdoctoral Research Associate under the supervision of Nathan Furey to perform research in seabird ecology, including identifying problems in detail; recording, analyzing and tabulating data obtained through lab, field, or quantitative research; recommending solutions where required; and reporting and publishing final results. Duties and Responsibilities: 20% Gather research data in the field and in the lab. 20% Assemble, organize, record, analyze and tabulate research data, relating to other work done in the field, as appropriate. 20% Write code to conduct data wrangling and analyses 20% Prepare recommendations for solutions of problems or other research findings in written form, either as formalized reports to supervisor or through publishing of results. 20% Collaborate with other team members on various lab projects Minimum Qualifications: Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: PhD in quantitative ecology, marine science, or related field Multiple years of experience working directly with seabirds, particularly in the northwest Atlantic  Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Quantitative ecology, marine science, or related field Knowledge of seabird ecology Strong background and experience in movement ecology and trophic ecology Understanding of marine systems, particularly the northwest Atlantic  Applicant Instructions: Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/Cover Letter section of your application: Resume/CV Cover Letter Contact Information for 3 Professional References Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration.  Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed.   Compensation Pay Range: $63,500.00/Annual Please be advised that this position is supported by external sponsor funding. As such, continued employment in this role is contingent upon the availability of those external funds. If, for any reason, the sponsor funding is reduced or discontinued, employment may be subject to modification or termination in accordance with applicable policies and procedures.  The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
