    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate_JR10407": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app A postdoctoral research associate is required in the Engineering for Agri-Environment Lab (https://sites.usnh.edu/eaelab/), led by Md Sazan Rahman, in the Department of Agriculture, Nutrition, and Food Systems at the University of New Hampshire, to carry out some of the key research in Controlled Environment Agriculture (CEA). The research activities include, among others, modeling and simulation of microclimate dynamics in controlled environment agriculture (CEA), precision monitoring and management of temperature and humidity, and the development of predictive environmental control frameworks to improve productivity and resource efficiency. The candidate will be supervised by Dr. Md Sazan Rahman in conducting the research and will collaborate with researchers and specialists across the University of New Hampshire, Cooperative Extension, and regional growers engaged in sustainable protected agriculture. Duties/Responsibilities The postdoctoral researcher will lead research on the advanced simulation of temperature and moisture dynamics in protected agriculture systems, encompassing moisture generation, transpiration, and dynamics in air and substrate. The postdoctoral researcher will develop and validate computational models of CEA heat and moisture transfer using platforms such as ANSYS Fluent, TRANSYS, COMSOL Multiphysics, and/or other relevant simulation software. The position also involves working with growers to provide technical assistance for on-farm environmental management and enriching the HVAC-related research program in the EAE lab.   Requirements Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience:  A Ph.D. in Agricultural Engineering, Environmental Engineering, Biosystems/Bioresource/Biological Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, or a related field. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Research and Outreach Skills: Strong research experience in modeling of heat and moisture transfer, environmental simulation, and microclimate management in protected agriculture. Experience with calibration of environmental sensors and systems integration, data collection, and analysis. Demonstrated record of publishing in peer-reviewed journals. Programming and Computing Skills: Experience using  TRANSYS, ANSYS Fluent , COMSOL Multiphysics, or similar platforms for thermal and fluid dynamics simulations. Strong programming skills in MATLAB, Python, R, or similar. Machine learning or data-driven modeling experience applied to environmental control systems is preferred.   Additional Experience: Proficiency with data acquisition systems (e.g., LabVIEW, CRBasic). Strong written and verbal communication skills. Experience designing and conducting laboratory and field experiments. Ability to manage multiple projects and supervise graduate and undergraduate students. Proven ability to meet deadlines. A valid driver’s license and willingness to travel regionally are preferred. Preferred Qualifications:   Any previous work/research experience in controlled environment agriculture (CEA) field will be an asset. Candidates who are already staying inside the USA will be preferred; however, international candidates will also be encouraged to apply. Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents - additional documents can be added to the Application Questions section of the application)   Resume/CV Cover Letter Research Statement   Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration. Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed.   Compensation Pay Range: $ 45,000-$51,000 The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate’s qualifications. Please be advised that this position is supported by external sponsor funding. As such, continued employment in this role is contingent upon the availability of those external funds. If, for any reason, the sponsor funding is reduced or discontinued, employment may be subject to modification or termination in accordance with applicable policies and procedures.  The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Associate-in-Fusarium-Genomics--Detection--and-Host-Range_JR10120": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Fusarium Genomics, Detection, and Host Range",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app Postdoctoral Research Associate in Fusarium Genomics, Detection, and Host Range Position Overview The University of New Hampshire (UNH) invites applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate to join a multidisciplinary project investigating a newly recognized Fusarium -associated disease of cold-hardy grapevines in the northern United States. The project integrates fungal genomics, plant pathology, molecular diagnostics, host-range studies, and grower surveys to advance understanding and management of this emerging disease. This is a full-time UNH appointment based at the USDA Agricultural Research Service (USDA-ARS), Cereal Crops Research Unit in Madison, Wisconsin. The successful candidate will work with Xiaochen Yuan (UNH), Kristi Gdanetz MacCready (USDA-ARS), and Suzanne Slack (UNH). Appointment Details Anticipated start date: January 4, 2027. Appointment length: One year initially, with the possibility of renewal for a second year contingent upon performance, continued funding, and project needs. Primary work location: Madison, Wisconsin. Travel: Approximately three short visits per year to Durham, New Hampshire, for research meetings, training, or project activities. Eligibility: This opportunity is open to U.S. citizens only. Appointment is subject to satisfactory completion of the required background investigation and/or fingerprint check and the ability to meet Tier 2 (T2) background investigation requirements for work at a federal research facility. Responsibilities The successful candidate will: Assemble, annotate, and analyze Fusarium genomes and conduct comparative genomic analyses across species and strains. Conduct host-range experiments involving grapevine, barley, wheat, maize, oats, and ryegrass. Investigate relationships between Fusarium populations associated with grapevines and cereal crops. Assist with a regional survey of cold-hardy grape growers across the northern United States. Contribute to the development and evaluation of PCR- and high-resolution melting-based diagnostic tools. Support studies of fungicide sensitivity and pruning or wound-management practices. Prepare manuscripts, reports, presentations, and extension materials, and present findings at scientific conferences, grower meetings, and extension events. Mentor undergraduate and graduate researchers as appropriate. Collaborate with project partners across participating institutions. Required Qualifications A Ph.D. in Plant Pathology, Microbiology, Mycology, Biology, Genomics, or a closely related field by the appointment start date. Candidates expecting to complete their Ph.D. by December 2026 are encouraged to apply . Demonstrated research experience in one or more of the following areas: fungal genomics, molecular diagnostics, fungal pathogens, plant–microbe interactions, or comparative genomics. Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research environment. Willingness and ability to participate in laboratory, greenhouse, field, and grower-oriented research. Ability to meet the federal facility access and background investigation requirements described above. Preferred Qualifications Experience in one or more of the following areas is preferred: Biology, taxonomy, phylogenetics, or population genomics of Fusarium or related fungal pathogens. Whole-genome sequencing, genome assembly and annotation, pangenome analysis, comparative genomics, or genome-wide association studies. Bioinformatics workflows using Linux, high-performance computing systems, Python, R, or related tools. Plant disease diagnostics, HRM analysis, or molecular assay development. Disease research involving grapes, cereal crops, or other specialty crops. Salary and Benefits The anticipated annual salary is $60,000–$65,000, commensurate with qualifications and experience. Benefits include medical, dental, and vision plan options; life and long-term disability insurance; retirement plan options; and paid time off, subject to university policies and eligibility requirements. Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents - If more than 5 documents are required, upload additional documents within the Application Questions section.) A cover letter describing their research background, technical expertise, and interest in the position. A curriculum vitae. A brief research statement describing experience relevant to fungal genomics, host-range experiments, plant pathology, bioinformatics, or grower-oriented research. Contact information for three professional references. Copies of transcripts. Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration. Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed. Review of applications will begin two weeks after the posting date and continue until the position is filled. Please be advised that this position is supported by external sponsor funding. As such, continued employment in this role is contingent upon the availability of those external funds. If, for any reason, the sponsor funding is reduced or discontinued, employment may be subject to modification or termination in accordance with applicable policies and procedures.  The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/University-of-New-Hampshire--Main-Campus/Clinical-Assistant-Professor_JR10162": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The Department of Occupational Therapy at the University of New Hampshire invites applications for a full-time Clinical Assistant Professor of Occupational Therapy with an anticipated start date of January 2027. The position is a 9-month academic year appointment with the opportunity for an additional part-time summer contract. The Department of Occupational Therapy is housed in the College of Health and Human Services within UNH. UNH is a research-high, liberal arts, land grant institution. UNH provides a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. Duties/Responsibilities: Teaching   Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in alignment with ACOTE standards, course specific objectives and student outcomes.  Develop and deliver course materials, assignments, exams, and clinical and applied learning activities  Contribute to curriculum quality through course preparation, assessment, and continuous improvement of instructional materials  Evaluate and document student progress and provide relevant and timely feedback to students.  Participate in evaluation of courses, curricula, programs, and accreditation activities.  Advise and mentor students, supporting academic progress and experiential learning  Support admissions processes at undergraduate and graduate level  Serve as a capstone project faculty mentor to OTD students  Fieldwork Assist the Academic Fieldwork Coordinator for the OT department with various fieldwork education processes across multiple OT programs. Coordinate student fieldwork placements, maintain clinical/community partnerships, and support effective fieldwork experiences. Collaborate with OT faculty and administrative teams to ensure compliance with ACOTE Standards and program requirements. Collect, analyze, and utilize fieldwork data to support curriculum development, assessment, and continuous quality improvement. Advise students regarding fieldwork readiness, professional behaviors, learning objectives, and successful participation in Level I and/or Level II fieldwork experiences. Support students in developing skills and strategies needed to meet site-specific expectations and competencies. Communicate with fieldwork educators, students, and campus stakeholders to promote effective supervision, evaluation, and student learning experiences. Ensure students meet required health, safety, compliance, and onboarding requirements prior to fieldwork participation. Participate in department initiatives, accreditation activities, curriculum planning, and student success efforts as it related to fieldwork. Service    Consistent with the service mission of the University, engage in service activities at university, departmental, college, and professional levels.   Requirements: Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: Terminal degree (PhD, OTD, ScD or similar) in occupational therapy At least 2 years of experience in OT clinical practice preferably in one or more of the following areas: adult mental health, adult physical disabilities, geriatrics, leadership in OT practice Required Licenses & Certifications: Active NH OT license or eligibility for NBCOT certification and eligibility for NH state OT license NH driver’s license or eligibility Preferred Qualifications: An occupation-focused perspective, with teaching and/or fieldwork experience preferred Professional experience as a fieldwork educator or documentation of training in the roles and responsibilities of a fieldwork educator. Experience working with college-age and non-traditional students. Skill in working with culturally diverse constituencies in the academic environment. Ability to cultivate external relationships with community leaders, institutions, and stakeholders. Experience teaching via online learning management systems Demonstrated ability to work with faculty, staff, and students as well as community groups of diverse academic, socioeconomic, cultural, sexual orientation, disability and ethnic backgrounds. Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents - additional documents can be added to the Application Questions section of the application)     Resume/CV Cover Letter Contact Information for 3 Professional References Teaching Philosophy   Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration. Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed. Compensation Pay Range: $ 75,000-$77,000 (Clinical Assistant Professor Rank) The University of New Hampshire is an R1 Carnegie classification research institution providing comprehensive, high-quality undergraduate and graduate programs of distinction. UNH is located in Durham on a 188-acre campus, 60 miles north of Boston and 8 miles from the Atlantic coast and is convenient to New Hampshire’s lakes and mountains. There is a student enrollment of 13,000 students, with a full-time faculty of over 600, offering 90 undergraduate and more than 70 graduate programs. The University actively promotes a dynamic learning environment in which qualified individuals of differing perspectives, life experiences, and cultural backgrounds pursue academic goals with mutual respect and shared inquiry. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Location: Durham Salary Grade: Faculty 01"
    }
